# One paragraph per section: what the section establishes and how, 40-90 words (110 with subsections, appendices
# 40-70). Every sentence sits in a span naming the 1-3 paragraphs (or figures) that establish it; the introduction's
# brief grounds on the introduction. Status ai-draft. Acknowledgements get no brief.
BRIEF = {
    "sect-1.intro": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-1.intro-p1 sect-1.intro-p4">Algebras of observables dressed to an observer give entropy differences in closed universes, but not the leading $O(1/G)$ constant, so universes cannot be compared.</span> <span data-covers="sect-1.intro-p5 sect-1.intro-p3">Witten proposed fixing it by defining entropy relative to the identity operator, if the no-boundary state is that identity; the paper argues it is.</span> <span data-covers="sect-1.intro-p7 sect-1.intro-p9">The reason is that the observer's worldline turns the no-boundary geometry into a wormhole joining bra and ket.</span> <span data-covers="sect-1.intro-p8 sect-1.intro-p10">The claim is checked in JT gravity, extended to horizons, and met by a no-go for KMS states beyond de Sitter.</span></p>""",

    "sect-2.obscomplete": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-2.obscomplete-p1">When the observer can see a whole Cauchy slice, the state of the universe is a density matrix on the full physical Hilbert space.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.1-p2">The observer's worldline cannot end, so the usual Hartle–Hawking cap is absent and the no-boundary state is a bra-ket wormhole.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.2-p1 sect-2.2-p3">That wormhole path integral is the one defining gravitational inner products, so $\rho_\text{NB}=\mathbb{1}$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-canonical_quant-p3 sec-canonical_quant-p5">In ADM variables the same statement reads $\rho_\text{NB}=\delta(H_\text{WDW})$, the rigging map that projects onto physical states.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.4topo-p3">Summing over topologies changes nothing, because inner products and the state sum over the same spacetimes.</span></p>""",

    "sect-2.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-2.1-p1">The boundary data are a Cauchy slice's metric, fields and observer, with the observer a heavy particle that barely backreacts.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.1-p2">A Hartle–Hawking cap would leave the worldline nowhere to end, so no pure no-boundary state exists; one must take a density matrix, with the worldline running from bra to ket.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.1-p2 sect-2.1-p3">The connected wormhole, of topology $\mathcal{M}\times I$, is then the leading contribution, and the state is mixed. No choice of Lorentzian, Euclidean or complex contour is needed.</span></p>""",

    "sect-2.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-2.2-p1">The gravitational inner product between two slices fills in all geometries joining them, exactly the path integral for $\rho_\text{NB}$; hence $\rho_\text{NB}=\mathbb{1}$.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.2-p2 sect-2.2-p3">The identity acts on the physical Hilbert space, so matrix products integrate over boundary data modulo diffeomorphisms, and squaring gives back the same wormhole: $\rho_\text{NB}^2=\rho_\text{NB}$.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.2-p4">In ordinary field theory the same gluing doubles the inverse temperature; gravity differs because its Hamiltonian vanishes.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.2-p5 sect-2.2-p6">The norm is often divergent, a weight; with a dominant saddle it is $e^{-I}$, which sets the entropy's constant.</span></p>""",

    "sec-canonical_quant": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-canonical_quant-p2 sec-canonical_quant-p3">In ADM form the lapse enters only as a multiplier of the Hamiltonian constraint, so integrating it over the real line gives $\rho_\text{NB}=\delta(H_\text{WDW})$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-canonical_quant-p3">Both signs of the lapse are kept: the result is Hermitian, and bra and ket enter on an equal footing.</span> <span data-covers="sec-canonical_quant-p5 sec-canonical_quant-p6">This delta function is the rigging map, the inner product on physical states, so it is the identity on the physical Hilbert space, and traces of physical operators agree.</span></p>""",

    "sect-2.4topo": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-2.4topo-p1 sect-2.4topo-p3">Topology change has no Hamiltonian description, but the path-integral argument survives it: inner products also sum over higher topologies, so the state stays the identity.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.4topo-p2">Disconnected contributions are excluded or suppressed by recent rules for observers; without such rules closed universes would have a one-dimensional Hilbert space.</span> <span data-covers="sect-2.4topo-p4 sect-2.4topo-p5">Baby universes leave the dimension unchanged, and the topology sum is what separates gravity from topological field theory.</span></p>""",

    "sect-4.JT": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-4.JT-p1 sect-4.JT-p2">In JT gravity with $\Lambda<0$ the closed universes are crunching cosmologies, and the Euclidean wormhole amplitudes are known exactly.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.1HJT-p1 sect-3.1HJT-p2">Canonically, physical states are labelled by the universe's maximal size $b$, with an observer's position removed by the momentum constraint.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT1empty-p2 sect-3.3ONBJT-p3">The double-trumpet wormhole, with or without an observer particle, reproduces the matrix elements of the identity on these states.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.3ONBJT-p4">The path integral thus imposes the momentum constraint too, and the no-boundary state is the trace.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT4nonpert-p2">With all topologies, the observer's Hilbert space is spanned by the $N$ energy eigenstates of one ensemble member.</span></p>""",

    "sect-3.1HJT": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-3.1HJT-p1">After gauge fixing, closed JT universes have a four-dimensional phase space with one Hamiltonian constraint, and physical states are labelled by the maximal size $b$.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.1HJT-p1">The Klein–Gordon inner product gives $\langle b_1|b_2\rangle=\delta(b_1-b_2)/b_1$, which fixes the identity's measure $b\,db$.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.1HJT-p2">An observer particle does not just add its own Hilbert space: spatial translations are gauge, so the momentum constraint leaves states $|b,k=0,q\rangle$.</span></p>""",

    "subsect-JT1empty": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsect-JT1empty-p2">Without an observer, but keeping the cylinder topology, the Euclidean wormhole is two trumpets glued along a geodesic of length $b$.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT1empty-p2">With asymptotic boundaries each trumpet equals the physical wavefunction $\psi_b$, so the amplitude is exactly $\int b\,db\,\psi_b\psi_b$, the identity's matrix element.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT1empty-p3">The WDW equation extends the match to finite boundaries.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT1empty-p4">Two classical solutions, one for each time order, connect bra and ket, which is why the lapse runs over both signs.</span></p>""",

    "sect-3.3ONBJT": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-3.3ONBJT-p1">With an observer particle on asymptotic boundaries, the wormhole amplitude is diagonal in energy, with a factor $\Gamma(\Delta)^2\Gamma(\Delta\pm2i\sqrt{E})/\Gamma(2\Delta)$.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.3ONBJT-p2 sect-3.3ONBJT-p3">The identity, with its momentum constraint written as an average over twists, is evaluated through geodesic lengths and winding propagators, and reproduces this amplitude.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.3ONBJT-p4">So the Euclidean path integral imposes the momentum constraint as well as the Hamiltonian one, which earlier calculations did not make manifest, and the no-boundary state is the trace.</span> <span data-covers="sect-3.3ONBJT-p5">The construction extends to $n$ particles.</span></p>""",

    "subsect-JT4nonpert": r"""<p><span data-covers="subsect-JT4nonpert-p1">With topology change there is no known Hamiltonian, so the path integral is read as a statement about Hilbert spaces.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT4nonpert-p2">In one member of the JT ensemble, without an observer bra and ket are uncorrelated, so the Hilbert space is one-dimensional.</span> <span data-covers="subsect-JT4nonpert-p2">With an observer a diagonal sum over the energies $E_i$ appears: the state is the identity on an $N$-dimensional space, a weight since $N=\infty$, normalizable in UV completions such as sine dilaton gravity.</span></p>""",

    "sect-3obshorizon": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-3obshorizon-p1">When the observer has a horizon, the global identity should restrict to the identity on the observer's subalgebra.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p3 section-4.1-p4">Algebraically, a trace is unique up to scale, so no-boundary expectation values are $\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}$ times the trace, and entropy acquires the definite constant $\log\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}$, to leading order minus the saddle's action.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.2-p2">A qubit model shows the diverging prefactor separating from a finite tracial state.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.3-p2 section-4.3-p4">A path integral with boundary data on the diamond's light cones gives $\rho_\text{NB}^2=\rho_\text{NB}$, since no conical excess forms.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.3-p6 section-4.1-p3">This agrees with the de Sitter trace and with traces for regions bounded by extremal surfaces.</span></p>""",

    "section-4.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.1-p1 section-4.1-p2">Perturbatively, each background is a superselection sector, and the observer's diamond carries an algebra whose global completions can differ.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p3">If that algebra is type I or II without center, its trace is unique up to scale, so $\langle a\rangle_\text{NB}=\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}\operatorname{Tr}(a)$; for de Sitter the factor is $e^{A/4G}$.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p4">The entropy, minus the relative entropy to $\rho_\text{NB}$, then has no additive ambiguity, and to leading order $S=-I_\mathcal{M}+O(1)$.</span></p>""",

    "section-4.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.2-p1">Bell pairs between left and right qubits give a state that is tracial for the right side, the analogue of de Sitter's Bunch–Davies state with an observer.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.2-p2">The unnormalized identity on both sides reduces to the same right-side state times $2^N$, a prefactor that diverges in the thermodynamic limit, as $\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}$ does.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.2-p2 section-4.2-p3">Adding an oscillator models type II$_\infty$, and a direct sum of sectors gives each its own prefactor.</span></p>""",

    "section-4.3": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.3-p1 section-4.3-p2">A region is invariantly defined by an eternal observer's diamond or by an extremal surface; the density matrix takes boundary data on its null boundaries.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.3-p3 section-4.3-p4">Squaring evolves between the two diamonds' data, and gravity smooths the edge, with no conical excess as in AdS replicas, so $\rho_\text{NB}^2=\rho_\text{NB}$.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.3-p5">The norm sets the leading entropy; it is the horizon area in de Sitter but not in general.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.3-p6 section-4.3-p7">For extremal regions the trace of Chen and Penington should be its $O(1)$ part; how the sphere appears on reduction is not understood.</span></p>""",

    "sect-nogo": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-nogo-p1">An explicit trace for an observer is known when matter is KMS on the worldline, as the Bunch–Davies state is in de Sitter.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.1kms-p2 sect-5.1kms-p3">For any other asymptotically de Sitter spacetime no such state exists: KMS forces worldline correlators to be Bunch–Davies ones, and those violate the short-distance commutator unless the curvature is de Sitter's.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.2constructive-p5">FLRW geometries built to be analytic and periodic in imaginary time fail too, through curvature singularities in the Euclidean section.</span> <span data-covers="sect-nogo-p1">This does not rule out a trace; it means building one needs more work.</span></p>""",

    "sect-5.1kms": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-5.1kms-p2">A worldline KMS correlator is periodic and analytic in a strip of imaginary time, and by the cosmic no-hair theorem it tends to the Bunch–Davies one, so Liouville's theorem makes them equal everywhere.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.1kms-p3">But the commutator's universal short-distance form, $R/48\pi$, matches the Bunch–Davies value $H^2/4\pi$ only at constant de Sitter curvature.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.1kms-p4">Equivalently, KMS needs a timelike isometry, and a stationary asymptotically de Sitter region is de Sitter.</span></p>""",

    "sect-5.2constructive": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-5.2constructive-p3">Demanding an analytic scale factor, periodic in imaginary time, with de Sitter asymptotics and a smooth Euclidean cap leaves a one-parameter family.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.2constructive-p3 sect-5.2constructive-p4">It is realized by a cosmological constant plus a gas of domain walls, whose throat narrows as their density grows.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.2constructive-p5">Conformal primaries then have Bunch–Davies correlators on the worldline, but the curvature diverges at the Euclidean poles, so descendants break KMS except in de Sitter.</span></p>""",

    "sect-5.conclusion": r"""<p><span data-covers="sect-5.conclusion-p2">No-boundary saddles generally give only a “weak KMS” property, which may suffice to construct the trace; a type III algebra is not excluded.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.conclusion-p3 sect-5.conclusion-p4">A test case: FLRW wormholes supported by axion flux, de Sitter at large times, whose observer sees everything above a critical flux.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.conclusion-p5">Their action, $I=-\frac{\pi}{2GH}(1-q)$, differs from the horizon area and stays of order $1/G$ where a type I algebra is expected.</span> <span data-covers="sect-5.conclusion-p6">Euclidean solutions winding several times have action unbounded below, so this saddle's dominance is unclear.</span></p>""",

    "app-cosmo": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-cosmo-p1">A simpler observer is a dynamical cosmological constant, an energy density coupled to JT gravity.</span> <span data-covers="app-cosmo-p1">Its Euclidean wormhole is the double trumpet times $\delta(\Lambda_1-\Lambda_2)$, and solving the WDW equation canonically gives the same identity operator, so path integral and canonical arguments agree without a hybrid step.</span></p>""",
}
